1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,287
Tell a friend to meet you in room
three, and they may still end up in the

2
00:00:04,287 --> 00:00:05,187
wrong place.

3
00:00:05,459 --> 00:00:06,649
Which room three?

4
00:00:06,649 --> 00:00:07,746
On which floor?

5
00:00:07,746 --> 00:00:12,024
A full address needs two parts, and
one part can never stand in for the

6
00:00:12,024 --> 00:00:12,924
other.

7
00:00:13,041 --> 00:00:15,821
Diya learnt this in the
college corridor today.

8
00:00:15,821 --> 00:00:18,566
She waited in room three
on the second floor.

9
00:00:18,566 --> 00:00:21,680
Her friend waited in room
three on the ground floor.

10
00:00:22,030 --> 00:00:24,295
Complex numbers work the same way.

11
00:00:24,295 --> 00:00:27,678
Each one is a single number
with two separate parts.

12
00:00:28,178 --> 00:00:30,417
Today we learn to read both parts.

13
00:00:31,067 --> 00:00:33,657
First, the definition,
straight from NCERT.

14
00:00:34,007 --> 00:00:38,771
A complex number is a number of the
form a plus i b, where a and b are

15
00:00:38,771 --> 00:00:39,798
real numbers.

16
00:00:40,198 --> 00:00:41,710
We often call it z.

17
00:00:41,710 --> 00:00:44,998
The real number a is
called the real part of z.

18
00:00:44,998 --> 00:00:46,772
We write Re z equals a.

19
00:00:47,172 --> 00:00:50,862
The real number b is called
the imaginary part of z.

20
00:00:50,862 --> 00:00:52,712
We write Im z equals b.

21
00:00:52,712 --> 00:00:55,193
Notice: b alone, without the i.

22
00:00:55,843 --> 00:00:59,206
For example, take z
equals two plus five i.

23
00:00:59,206 --> 00:01:00,976
Its real part is two.

24
00:01:00,976 --> 00:01:03,078
Its imaginary part is five.

25
00:01:03,478 --> 00:01:05,915
Now picture the two parts as an address.

26
00:01:06,265 --> 00:01:09,618
The real part a tells you
how far along the street.

27
00:01:09,618 --> 00:01:12,865
The imaginary part b tells
you how many floors up.

28
00:01:13,265 --> 00:01:14,165
Watch.

29
00:01:13,846 --> 00:01:16,355
Let a count from minus three to three.

30
00:01:16,355 --> 00:01:20,524
The point slides along the street,
and only the real part changes.

31
00:01:20,924 --> 00:01:22,463
Now let b count up.

32
00:01:22,463 --> 00:01:26,146
The point climbs, and only
the imaginary part changes.

33
00:01:26,546 --> 00:01:29,703
Two numbers, two directions,
one complex number.

34
00:01:29,703 --> 00:01:32,541
A later episode turns
this into a full map.

35
00:01:33,191 --> 00:01:34,309
Side by side.

36
00:01:34,309 --> 00:01:37,892
If b is zero, the point
sits right on the street.

37
00:01:37,892 --> 00:01:40,553
z equals a, an ordinary real number.

38
00:01:40,553 --> 00:01:42,313
We call it purely real.

39
00:01:42,713 --> 00:01:47,496
If a is zero and b is not zero, the
point sits straight above or below

40
00:01:47,496 --> 00:01:48,399
the start.

41
00:01:48,399 --> 00:01:49,581
z equals i b.

42
00:01:49,581 --> 00:01:51,604
We call it purely imaginary.

43
00:01:52,204 --> 00:01:55,620
So every real number is
also a complex number.

44
00:01:55,620 --> 00:01:57,786
Seven is seven plus zero i.

45
00:01:58,436 --> 00:01:59,531
Now you try.

46
00:01:59,531 --> 00:02:02,858
Is i squared purely real,
or purely imaginary?

47
00:02:02,858 --> 00:02:04,165
Pause and think.

48
00:02:07,365 --> 00:02:08,409
Purely real!

49
00:02:08,409 --> 00:02:12,518
i squared is minus one, which
is minus one plus zero i.

50
00:02:13,168 --> 00:02:17,622
Before you read the parts, always
tidy the number into a plus i b.

51
00:02:17,622 --> 00:02:18,522
Watch.

52
00:02:18,577 --> 00:02:19,494
Step one.

53
00:02:19,494 --> 00:02:23,129
Take z equals three plus
five i minus i squared.

54
00:02:23,879 --> 00:02:24,779
Step two.

55
00:02:24,686 --> 00:02:28,449
i squared is minus one, so
minus i squared is plus one.

56
00:02:29,199 --> 00:02:30,137
Step three.

57
00:02:30,137 --> 00:02:32,226
Group the real terms together.

58
00:02:32,226 --> 00:02:33,958
Three plus one is four.

59
00:02:34,708 --> 00:02:35,668
Step four.

60
00:02:35,668 --> 00:02:38,008
So z equals four plus five i.

61
00:02:38,008 --> 00:02:40,700
Re z is four, and Im z is five.

62
00:02:41,450 --> 00:02:42,724
Remember this.

63
00:02:42,724 --> 00:02:43,902
Re z is a.

64
00:02:43,902 --> 00:02:45,080
Im z is b.

65
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:46,972
Both are real numbers.

66
00:02:47,672 --> 00:02:49,408
Now, one worked example.

67
00:02:49,758 --> 00:02:55,380
z equals k minus two, plus k squared
minus nine, times i, where k is real.

68
00:02:55,380 --> 00:02:57,667
For which k is z purely real?

69
00:02:57,667 --> 00:03:00,278
For which k is it purely imaginary?

70
00:03:00,778 --> 00:03:01,693
Step one.

71
00:03:01,693 --> 00:03:03,009
Read the parts.

72
00:03:03,009 --> 00:03:04,804
Re z is k minus two.

73
00:03:04,804 --> 00:03:07,168
Im z is k squared minus nine.

74
00:03:07,918 --> 00:03:08,818
Step two.

75
00:03:08,813 --> 00:03:11,236
Purely real means Im z is zero.

76
00:03:11,236 --> 00:03:15,177
k squared equals nine, so
k is three or minus three.

77
00:03:15,927 --> 00:03:17,030
Step three.

78
00:03:17,030 --> 00:03:19,993
Purely imaginary means Re z is zero.

79
00:03:19,993 --> 00:03:21,541
So k equals two.

80
00:03:22,291 --> 00:03:23,301
Step four.

81
00:03:23,301 --> 00:03:26,123
Check that Im z is not zero there.

82
00:03:26,123 --> 00:03:28,573
Four minus nine is minus five.

83
00:03:28,573 --> 00:03:30,733
So z equals minus five i.

84
00:03:31,483 --> 00:03:33,942
On the address board, watch k slide.

85
00:03:33,942 --> 00:03:37,874
The point crosses the street
twice, and the upright line once.

86
00:03:38,524 --> 00:03:39,990
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:45,186
Many students say the imaginary part
of three minus four i is minus four

88
00:03:45,186 --> 00:03:46,086
i.

89
00:03:46,029 --> 00:03:50,045
But the imaginary part is the
real number in front of i.

90
00:03:50,045 --> 00:03:52,964
Im of three minus four i is minus four.

91
00:03:52,964 --> 00:03:53,864
No i.

92
00:03:54,274 --> 00:03:55,801
Where is this asked?

93
00:03:55,801 --> 00:04:00,595
In JEE Main, for example in January
twenty nineteen: find the angles that

94
00:04:00,595 --> 00:04:02,746
make a number purely imaginary.

95
00:04:02,746 --> 00:04:05,799
And in PUC boards, from
this NCERT section.

96
00:04:06,399 --> 00:04:07,718
Exam level now.

97
00:04:07,718 --> 00:04:11,797
For how many integers k is
this number purely imaginary?

98
00:04:11,797 --> 00:04:13,171
Pause and solve.

99
00:04:18,571 --> 00:04:20,047
The answer is B.

100
00:04:20,047 --> 00:04:20,986
Just one.

101
00:04:21,486 --> 00:04:24,963
Purely imaginary needs
the real part to be zero.

102
00:04:24,963 --> 00:04:29,192
k squared minus four is zero,
so k is two or minus two.

103
00:04:29,692 --> 00:04:33,242
It also needs the imaginary
part to be non zero.

104
00:04:33,242 --> 00:04:37,196
At k equal to two, four
minus ten plus six is zero.

105
00:04:37,196 --> 00:04:39,567
So z is zero, and two fails.

106
00:04:40,067 --> 00:04:44,058
At k equal to minus two, the
imaginary part is twenty.

107
00:04:44,058 --> 00:04:45,940
So only minus two works.

108
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:47,996
Here is your recap.

109
00:04:47,996 --> 00:04:49,493
Screenshot this card.

110
00:04:52,693 --> 00:04:55,530
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:04:55,930 --> 00:04:56,830
One.

112
00:04:56,511 --> 00:05:01,122
What are Re z and Im z for z
equals minus five plus two i?

113
00:05:02,022 --> 00:05:02,922
Two.

114
00:05:02,507 --> 00:05:04,856
Is the number nine a complex number?

115
00:05:04,856 --> 00:05:05,756
Why?

116
00:05:06,241 --> 00:05:07,141
Three.

117
00:05:06,911 --> 00:05:11,962
For which k is k minus one, plus
k plus one times i, purely real?

118
00:05:13,062 --> 00:05:16,663
Next time: two moves on a
map, one after the other.

119
00:05:16,663 --> 00:05:20,235
We add, subtract, multiply
and divide complex numbers.
